1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:03,325
In this video, we'll
do an overview of

2
00:00:03,325 --> 00:00:06,515
Agile as a software
development methodology.

3
00:00:06,515 --> 00:00:11,500
We want to do an overview of
what agile development is

4
00:00:11,500 --> 00:00:13,135
and also how we will map

5
00:00:13,135 --> 00:00:17,540
Agile roles for the
course final project.

6
00:00:18,670 --> 00:00:20,890
Agile is described in

7
00:00:20,890 --> 00:00:23,545
the Agile Manifesto for
software development.

8
00:00:23,545 --> 00:00:25,750
There are a few different
things that are described in

9
00:00:25,750 --> 00:00:28,930
the manifesto in terms
of what the goal

10
00:00:28,930 --> 00:00:29,950
of Agile is or

11
00:00:29,950 --> 00:00:32,890
what the motivations methodology

12
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:34,790
for Agile development are.

13
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:37,000
Agile favors individuals and

14
00:00:37,000 --> 00:00:39,340
interactions over
processes and tools,

15
00:00:39,340 --> 00:00:42,155
so trying to keep things
more lightweight.

16
00:00:42,155 --> 00:00:43,640
It favors

17
00:00:43,640 --> 00:00:46,520
working software over
comprehensive documentation.

18
00:00:46,520 --> 00:00:48,710
Again, you'll have
documentation about what you're

19
00:00:48,710 --> 00:00:51,095
doing for a given activity,

20
00:00:51,095 --> 00:00:52,970
but it's less about

21
00:00:52,970 --> 00:00:54,950
trying to create that from
the beginning and try to

22
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:56,090
implement to that and more

23
00:00:56,090 --> 00:00:57,470
about trying to
keep things fluid

24
00:00:57,470 --> 00:01:00,725
and flexible as you're
doing the implementation.

25
00:01:00,725 --> 00:01:03,350
It's more about
customer collaboration

26
00:01:03,350 --> 00:01:04,819
over contract negotiation.

27
00:01:04,819 --> 00:01:08,180
It's a realization that things

28
00:01:08,180 --> 00:01:11,510
change and your interaction
with your customers,

29
00:01:11,510 --> 00:01:13,010
internal or external,

30
00:01:13,010 --> 00:01:15,635
is going to change as
the project progresses.

31
00:01:15,635 --> 00:01:17,600
As people see
things implemented,

32
00:01:17,600 --> 00:01:18,650
they're going to
change their mind

33
00:01:18,650 --> 00:01:19,910
about what they actually wanted.

34
00:01:19,910 --> 00:01:22,880
Agile tries to be able
to take advantage

35
00:01:22,880 --> 00:01:26,270
of the shorter
iteration times and

36
00:01:26,270 --> 00:01:30,229
less predefined work and more

37
00:01:30,229 --> 00:01:31,910
ability to change and adapt as

38
00:01:31,910 --> 00:01:34,190
you continue with the project.

39
00:01:34,190 --> 00:01:36,485
It's more about
responding to change

40
00:01:36,485 --> 00:01:37,550
over building

41
00:01:37,550 --> 00:01:40,295
a really detailed plan
and following the plan.

42
00:01:40,295 --> 00:01:44,930
Agile is not necessarily
something that I would say

43
00:01:44,930 --> 00:01:46,520
you definitely want to do on

44
00:01:46,520 --> 00:01:48,995
every project or that you
definitely want to follow.

45
00:01:48,995 --> 00:01:51,350
The main reason I'm talking
about Agile in this course is

46
00:01:51,350 --> 00:01:55,505
because it's a popular software
development methodology.

47
00:01:55,505 --> 00:01:57,110
A lot of people use some parts

48
00:01:57,110 --> 00:01:58,730
of Agile in software development

49
00:01:58,730 --> 00:01:59,930
and so it's a
common thing to get

50
00:01:59,930 --> 00:02:01,970
asked about in interviews.

51
00:02:01,970 --> 00:02:02,990
I'm really just
trying to give you

52
00:02:02,990 --> 00:02:04,475
a little bit of an overview here

53
00:02:04,475 --> 00:02:05,960
and a little bit of
an experience in

54
00:02:05,960 --> 00:02:08,460
an Agile like environment.

55
00:02:09,290 --> 00:02:12,580
One of the parts of Agile
that we'll be using in

56
00:02:12,580 --> 00:02:16,195
the final project is the
Daily Stand-up or Scrum.

57
00:02:16,195 --> 00:02:18,850
The idea behind the
Scrum is really

58
00:02:18,850 --> 00:02:22,840
just a process framework
for addressing problems.

59
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:24,205
Problems are going to happen

60
00:02:24,205 --> 00:02:25,885
as part of a
development process.

61
00:02:25,885 --> 00:02:27,580
The Scrum is where
you talk about what

62
00:02:27,580 --> 00:02:30,830
the problems are and how you're
going to deal with them.

63
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:33,820
A Scrum team is meant
to be a small team

64
00:02:33,820 --> 00:02:36,085
of people that's
flexible and adaptable,

65
00:02:36,085 --> 00:02:38,725
can change based on what
the new problems are for

66
00:02:38,725 --> 00:02:43,010
a given day and can reorient
their priorities quickly.

67
00:02:43,010 --> 00:02:46,960
A Scrum team is made up of
a few different people.

68
00:02:46,960 --> 00:02:48,805
One is the product owner,

69
00:02:48,805 --> 00:02:51,015
and the product owner
is really the one that

70
00:02:51,015 --> 00:02:52,580
figures out all the backlog

71
00:02:52,580 --> 00:02:53,915
of things that
you're going to do,

72
00:02:53,915 --> 00:02:55,745
what's the most important
thing to be doing?

73
00:02:55,745 --> 00:02:56,840
What's the most
important thing to

74
00:02:56,840 --> 00:02:58,610
include in the next sprint?

75
00:02:58,610 --> 00:02:59,750
Really is going to be providing

76
00:02:59,750 --> 00:03:01,070
the voice of the end users.

77
00:03:01,070 --> 00:03:03,185
Whoever is going to be
using your product,

78
00:03:03,185 --> 00:03:06,080
the product owner is responsible
for knowing what they

79
00:03:06,080 --> 00:03:09,310
collectively want or what
their highest priorities are.

80
00:03:09,310 --> 00:03:11,030
Then there's the
development team,

81
00:03:11,030 --> 00:03:13,490
which is probably going to
be you as the developer

82
00:03:13,490 --> 00:03:14,960
and the goal of

83
00:03:14,960 --> 00:03:16,560
the development team
is to deliver "Done."

84
00:03:16,560 --> 00:03:19,260
We'll talk more about what it
means to deliver it "Done."

85
00:03:19,260 --> 00:03:21,170
Usually on a Scrum
team that's going to

86
00:03:21,170 --> 00:03:23,165
be between three
and nine members.

87
00:03:23,165 --> 00:03:24,560
If you get to more
than nine members,

88
00:03:24,560 --> 00:03:25,790
it gets a little
harder to make it

89
00:03:25,790 --> 00:03:27,320
flexible and adaptive and so

90
00:03:27,320 --> 00:03:28,400
a lot of times you'll split into

91
00:03:28,400 --> 00:03:30,450
different Scrum
teams at that point.

92
00:03:30,450 --> 00:03:32,330
If there's less
than three members,

93
00:03:32,330 --> 00:03:34,250
it might be a little bit
more overhead than you need

94
00:03:34,250 --> 00:03:37,015
in order to really
do Agile Scrum.

95
00:03:37,015 --> 00:03:39,740
Then there's also
a Scrum master who

96
00:03:39,740 --> 00:03:41,720
is the one that's
enforcing the rules and

97
00:03:41,720 --> 00:03:43,655
coaching all the members on how

98
00:03:43,655 --> 00:03:47,280
you want to do Agile process.

99
00:03:47,570 --> 00:03:50,985
When you talk about Agile,

100
00:03:50,985 --> 00:03:54,450
there's analogy that
gets brought up often,

101
00:03:54,450 --> 00:03:56,475
that's about pigs and chickens.

102
00:03:56,475 --> 00:03:59,000
There's a way to think

103
00:03:59,000 --> 00:04:01,640
about who the
different roles are on

104
00:04:01,640 --> 00:04:03,590
an Agile team in terms of

105
00:04:03,590 --> 00:04:06,535
this cartoon that relates
to a pig and a chicken.

106
00:04:06,535 --> 00:04:08,390
In the cartoon, there's

107
00:04:08,390 --> 00:04:10,970
a chicken that's
talking to a pig about,

108
00:04:10,970 --> 00:04:13,730
hey, let's get together
and open a restaurant.

109
00:04:13,730 --> 00:04:15,080
A pig saying, okay, well, what

110
00:04:15,080 --> 00:04:16,745
would you want to call it?

111
00:04:16,745 --> 00:04:18,140
The chicken says,
well, how about

112
00:04:18,140 --> 00:04:19,730
if we call it Ham and Eggs?

113
00:04:19,730 --> 00:04:22,430
The pig says, no,
that's not a good idea.

114
00:04:22,430 --> 00:04:24,710
I don't like that because
I'd be committed,

115
00:04:24,710 --> 00:04:25,810
but you'd only be involved.

116
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:28,010
In other words, the
pig is committed in

117
00:04:28,010 --> 00:04:30,450
that there's no coming
back from the ham.

118
00:04:30,450 --> 00:04:32,600
But the chicken's just

119
00:04:32,600 --> 00:04:35,300
involved because it's
involved with making the egg,

120
00:04:35,300 --> 00:04:38,395
but not necessarily as
committed as the pig is.

121
00:04:38,395 --> 00:04:40,610
The pigs and chicken analogy

122
00:04:40,610 --> 00:04:41,900
describes the different roles of

123
00:04:41,900 --> 00:04:43,400
the people that are
on the Scrum team.

124
00:04:43,400 --> 00:04:45,950
The product owner,
the development team,

125
00:04:45,950 --> 00:04:48,305
and the Scrum master
are usually all pigs,

126
00:04:48,305 --> 00:04:50,915
in the sense that if
the project goes south,

127
00:04:50,915 --> 00:04:53,225
their jobs are on the line.

128
00:04:53,225 --> 00:04:56,970
It's directly going
to reflect on them.

129
00:04:58,030 --> 00:05:00,320
This might be counterintuitive,

130
00:05:00,320 --> 00:05:04,790
but the management is often
a chicken in this process,

131
00:05:04,790 --> 00:05:07,430
so they have a
reason to weigh in,

132
00:05:07,430 --> 00:05:08,990
but they are
probably involved in

133
00:05:08,990 --> 00:05:10,490
multiple projects or they're

134
00:05:10,490 --> 00:05:12,370
not directly involved
in this one.

135
00:05:12,370 --> 00:05:15,845
They're in the
Agile methodology.

136
00:05:15,845 --> 00:05:20,000
Their weight is
somewhat less than

137
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:21,770
the people who are the pigs that

138
00:05:21,770 --> 00:05:25,050
are committed to the project.

139
00:05:27,200 --> 00:05:31,175
If we try to map these Agile
roles to our final project,

140
00:05:31,175 --> 00:05:33,110
it's not going to be
perfect because we're

141
00:05:33,110 --> 00:05:35,075
not really an Agile
development team.

142
00:05:35,075 --> 00:05:37,700
But if you'd want
to try to map them

143
00:05:37,700 --> 00:05:40,535
to what it would be in
the final project role,

144
00:05:40,535 --> 00:05:42,290
the product owner
would essentially

145
00:05:42,290 --> 00:05:44,060
be a combination of you,

146
00:05:44,060 --> 00:05:47,150
student assistants,
and peer reviewers.

147
00:05:47,150 --> 00:05:48,875
The development team
is going to be you,

148
00:05:48,875 --> 00:05:50,360
you're going to be
doing the development.

149
00:05:50,360 --> 00:05:51,920
Then the Scrum master will

150
00:05:51,920 --> 00:05:53,840
be myself or the
student assistants,

151
00:05:53,840 --> 00:05:57,085
whoever's hosting the Scrum
and enforcing the rules.

152
00:05:57,085 --> 00:05:58,550
A lot of times what
you're doing as

153
00:05:58,550 --> 00:06:01,745
a Scrum master is just cutting
people off and saying,

154
00:06:01,745 --> 00:06:03,409
let's just keep to the details,

155
00:06:03,409 --> 00:06:06,305
keep the report short
and to the point,

156
00:06:06,305 --> 00:06:08,730
and move on to the next report.

157
00:06:09,510 --> 00:06:13,420
We talked before about a
backlog and organizing

158
00:06:13,420 --> 00:06:15,190
the backlog as something

159
00:06:15,190 --> 00:06:17,425
that the product
owner would be doing.

160
00:06:17,425 --> 00:06:19,285
When we talk about
a product backlog,

161
00:06:19,285 --> 00:06:20,620
it's really an ordered list of

162
00:06:20,620 --> 00:06:22,585
everything that needs
to be in the product.

163
00:06:22,585 --> 00:06:24,160
That could be
features, functions,

164
00:06:24,160 --> 00:06:27,025
requirements,
enhancements, fixes.

165
00:06:27,025 --> 00:06:30,100
It's going to include things
like the description,

166
00:06:30,100 --> 00:06:32,275
what needs to get
done, the order,

167
00:06:32,275 --> 00:06:34,480
which things are the
highest priority

168
00:06:34,480 --> 00:06:35,560
to do and what should be done

169
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:38,905
first and then estimate which
would be development time,

170
00:06:38,905 --> 00:06:43,135
cost, in terms of materials,
or personnel cost.

171
00:06:43,135 --> 00:06:44,620
Then the ultimate value,

172
00:06:44,620 --> 00:06:47,215
which is going to help
prioritize things like order.

173
00:06:47,215 --> 00:06:50,155
Again, the product owner is
responsible for the backlog,

174
00:06:50,155 --> 00:06:52,315
responsible for keeping
that backlog up to date,

175
00:06:52,315 --> 00:06:54,250
deciding what should be worked

176
00:06:54,250 --> 00:06:57,100
on next in the current sprint.

177
00:06:57,100 --> 00:06:59,785
This might seem
counterintuitive,

178
00:06:59,785 --> 00:07:01,105
but a product backlog,

179
00:07:01,105 --> 00:07:02,290
as long as a product is still

180
00:07:02,290 --> 00:07:03,925
alive is actually
never complete.

181
00:07:03,925 --> 00:07:05,680
You always have
more things to do

182
00:07:05,680 --> 00:07:07,930
in a product backlog than
you can ever get done.

183
00:07:07,930 --> 00:07:09,415
As you're completing a sprint,

184
00:07:09,415 --> 00:07:12,025
you've got two or three
sprints at least ahead

185
00:07:12,025 --> 00:07:14,830
of items in the backlog
that you're organizing,

186
00:07:14,830 --> 00:07:16,810
adding detail to, and

187
00:07:16,810 --> 00:07:20,870
you're never actually
completing your backlog.

188
00:07:21,530 --> 00:07:24,705
In the whole list of
the product backlog,

189
00:07:24,705 --> 00:07:26,100
I mentioned sprints as

190
00:07:26,100 --> 00:07:29,505
these subsets of intervals
where you're going to be doing

191
00:07:29,505 --> 00:07:32,370
some specific development
task or you're going

192
00:07:32,370 --> 00:07:34,540
to aim toward implementing

193
00:07:34,540 --> 00:07:36,820
some specific part
of the project.

194
00:07:36,820 --> 00:07:38,620
You take some subset of

195
00:07:38,620 --> 00:07:39,850
your product backlog and you'd

196
00:07:39,850 --> 00:07:41,740
select that for a given sprint.

197
00:07:41,740 --> 00:07:43,555
The sprint length would be

198
00:07:43,555 --> 00:07:45,910
some varied time
based on project,

199
00:07:45,910 --> 00:07:48,715
but usually measured
in one or two weeks,

200
00:07:48,715 --> 00:07:51,820
usually not a month or longer.

201
00:07:51,820 --> 00:07:54,160
The sprint backlog really has

202
00:07:54,160 --> 00:07:55,390
the functionality
that you want to

203
00:07:55,390 --> 00:07:56,830
deliver in the next increment,

204
00:07:56,830 --> 00:07:58,675
where that increment
is the sprint.

205
00:07:58,675 --> 00:08:00,640
It's the work that's needed to

206
00:08:00,640 --> 00:08:02,650
deliver a new done increment.

207
00:08:02,650 --> 00:08:05,080
I mentioned what done
means in a second.

208
00:08:05,080 --> 00:08:07,180
There's enough detail then in

209
00:08:07,180 --> 00:08:09,895
the sprint backlog for
each one of the tasks,

210
00:08:09,895 --> 00:08:11,530
such that in the daily Scrum,

211
00:08:11,530 --> 00:08:14,920
you can say, I made this amount
of progress on this task.

212
00:08:14,920 --> 00:08:16,960
In other words, we're
breaking up tasks into

213
00:08:16,960 --> 00:08:19,000
small enough pieces that

214
00:08:19,000 --> 00:08:21,399
in a week or a
two-week timeframe,

215
00:08:21,399 --> 00:08:23,680
we're going to have
measurable progress

216
00:08:23,680 --> 00:08:25,705
on each one of the tasks.

217
00:08:25,705 --> 00:08:27,370
We'll be able to report

218
00:08:27,370 --> 00:08:29,305
the status of that
progress in daily Scrum.

219
00:08:29,305 --> 00:08:30,520
You don't want your tasks to

220
00:08:30,520 --> 00:08:31,990
be so long that
they're going to take

221
00:08:31,990 --> 00:08:33,430
months to complete and it's

222
00:08:33,430 --> 00:08:35,050
really hard to measure
what you're doing.

223
00:08:35,050 --> 00:08:36,250
You want to try to
split them up into

224
00:08:36,250 --> 00:08:37,630
something smaller that you can

225
00:08:37,630 --> 00:08:40,750
measure whether you're
progressing in a daily Scrum.

226
00:08:40,750 --> 00:08:43,650
I've mentioned a couple times

227
00:08:43,650 --> 00:08:46,380
about done and what
done means in Agile.

228
00:08:46,380 --> 00:08:48,795
There's a definition of done,

229
00:08:48,795 --> 00:08:52,410
which we'll refer to with
DOD as an abbreviation.

230
00:08:52,410 --> 00:08:55,305
Where it's really important
that all the team members

231
00:08:55,305 --> 00:08:57,090
understand what done means

232
00:08:57,090 --> 00:08:58,935
in the context of
a backlog item.

233
00:08:58,935 --> 00:09:01,505
You as a developer
might have one idea

234
00:09:01,505 --> 00:09:04,855
what done means for your
code implementation,

235
00:09:04,855 --> 00:09:06,190
but the product owner might have

236
00:09:06,190 --> 00:09:08,410
a different idea of
what done means in

237
00:09:08,410 --> 00:09:09,820
terms of what they expect to

238
00:09:09,820 --> 00:09:12,070
see in the product
functionality.

239
00:09:12,070 --> 00:09:13,360
You need to make
sure that there's

240
00:09:13,360 --> 00:09:15,175
no disagreement there or

241
00:09:15,175 --> 00:09:17,350
the product owner is not
going to be happy with

242
00:09:17,350 --> 00:09:20,095
the result that comes out
of the development team.

243
00:09:20,095 --> 00:09:22,600
Having a definition of done

244
00:09:22,600 --> 00:09:24,250
of what's going to be
delivered is really

245
00:09:24,250 --> 00:09:26,635
important for that transparency

246
00:09:26,635 --> 00:09:28,840
and avoiding miscommunication.

247
00:09:28,840 --> 00:09:30,820
Another thing to keep
in mind about Agile

248
00:09:30,820 --> 00:09:32,500
is that every sprint increment,

249
00:09:32,500 --> 00:09:33,610
we want it to be usable.

250
00:09:33,610 --> 00:09:35,920
We don't want to have
a feature that we

251
00:09:35,920 --> 00:09:38,980
had in the last sprint be

252
00:09:38,980 --> 00:09:42,190
affected by some other feature
that we tried to add that

253
00:09:42,190 --> 00:09:43,420
was partially added that

254
00:09:43,420 --> 00:09:45,595
broke something that
used to be working.

255
00:09:45,595 --> 00:09:47,530
The idea is every sprint we

256
00:09:47,530 --> 00:09:49,270
deliver something
new that's usable,

257
00:09:49,270 --> 00:09:50,530
that the product owner who's

258
00:09:50,530 --> 00:09:51,775
interacting with the product,

259
00:09:51,775 --> 00:09:53,530
with the voice of
the end user can

260
00:09:53,530 --> 00:09:56,960
actually use and is functional.

261
00:09:57,150 --> 00:10:00,610
You need to define your
definition of done such

262
00:10:00,610 --> 00:10:01,900
that you'll end up with a usable

263
00:10:01,900 --> 00:10:03,265
product at the end
of the sprint.

264
00:10:03,265 --> 00:10:06,310
Even if a complete feature
is not implemented,

265
00:10:06,310 --> 00:10:08,425
you'd have some subset of
the feature implemented,

266
00:10:08,425 --> 00:10:09,970
and more importantly,
you won't have

267
00:10:09,970 --> 00:10:11,560
broken something that was

268
00:10:11,560 --> 00:10:12,970
implemented in a previous

269
00:10:12,970 --> 00:10:15,745
release or in a previous sprint.

270
00:10:15,745 --> 00:10:19,540
As you get further
along in the project,

271
00:10:19,540 --> 00:10:21,250
in the beginning of the
project, your definition

272
00:10:21,250 --> 00:10:22,780
of done might be pretty loose.

273
00:10:22,780 --> 00:10:24,910
But as the project
gets further along,

274
00:10:24,910 --> 00:10:27,010
it's a little bit more
easy to add detail

275
00:10:27,010 --> 00:10:29,860
about what actually
is it means to be

276
00:10:29,860 --> 00:10:32,080
done given this current state of

277
00:10:32,080 --> 00:10:33,880
the feature and what needs

278
00:10:33,880 --> 00:10:36,770
to be added in this
particular sprint.

279
00:10:37,740 --> 00:10:40,990
The daily stand-up that
will incorporate as

280
00:10:40,990 --> 00:10:43,915
a part of our Agile review
for the final project.

281
00:10:43,915 --> 00:10:46,315
You really need to think
about what did I do,

282
00:10:46,315 --> 00:10:48,310
if you're thinking about from

283
00:10:48,310 --> 00:10:49,750
a full-time work perspective and

284
00:10:49,750 --> 00:10:51,535
you're working five days a week;

285
00:10:51,535 --> 00:10:55,555
what did I do yesterday toward
my current sprint goal?

286
00:10:55,555 --> 00:10:57,490
Also, what am I
going to do today

287
00:10:57,490 --> 00:10:59,485
towards the current sprint goal?

288
00:10:59,485 --> 00:11:02,050
Then importantly,
what, if anything,

289
00:11:02,050 --> 00:11:03,550
is blocking my progress

290
00:11:03,550 --> 00:11:05,890
towards achieving my
portion of the sprint goal.

291
00:11:05,890 --> 00:11:07,660
This is part of where the Scrum

292
00:11:07,660 --> 00:11:09,520
turns into the
problem-solving session.

293
00:11:09,520 --> 00:11:12,580
Because if someone's
blocked on something else,

294
00:11:12,580 --> 00:11:13,930
then you work as a team to

295
00:11:13,930 --> 00:11:15,505
figure out how to get
people unblocked.

296
00:11:15,505 --> 00:11:18,955
Maybe it's part of the
work that's blocking them,

297
00:11:18,955 --> 00:11:20,155
they could do it themselves,

298
00:11:20,155 --> 00:11:21,385
or maybe they could
work on something

299
00:11:21,385 --> 00:11:22,660
else that's blocking

300
00:11:22,660 --> 00:11:23,740
the person that needs to get

301
00:11:23,740 --> 00:11:25,975
the task done that's
blocking them.

302
00:11:25,975 --> 00:11:28,840
That's part of the conversation

303
00:11:28,840 --> 00:11:32,090
that you have in
the Scrum stand-up.

304
00:11:32,190 --> 00:11:34,870
The blocking issue
could be something

305
00:11:34,870 --> 00:11:37,090
like a portion that's
not yet complete.

306
00:11:37,090 --> 00:11:39,730
It could be a bug, that
means you can't test or

307
00:11:39,730 --> 00:11:41,680
implement whatever part of

308
00:11:41,680 --> 00:11:43,435
code that you need
to for your sprint.

309
00:11:43,435 --> 00:11:46,090
Or it also could be like
an external problem,

310
00:11:46,090 --> 00:11:48,580
like I placed an order and
it hasn't gotten here yet,

311
00:11:48,580 --> 00:11:50,425
and so I can't do
whatever testing or

312
00:11:50,425 --> 00:11:52,435
implementation I needed to do.

313
00:11:52,435 --> 00:11:54,190
Or maybe if you're
in the software team

314
00:11:54,190 --> 00:11:55,495
and the hardware is not working,

315
00:11:55,495 --> 00:11:57,115
this is something that
you're blocked on,

316
00:11:57,115 --> 00:12:00,080
an external thing from
a different team.